PART 1 – Understanding GitHub
1. What is GitHub?
Start by asking your students:

"We have learned how to create repositories, commit files, create branches and merge changes using Git. But how can another developer access our code?"

Suppose you create a bakery website on your laptop.

Your project looks like this:

the-fairy-bake/
│
├── index.html
├── about.html
├── contact.html
├── style.css
└── script.js

All your project files and Git history are currently stored on your computer.

Your classmates cannot automatically access this repository.

You need a platform where you can upload, store and share your Git repository.

That platform is GitHub.

Definition:

GitHub is a cloud-based platform that hosts Git repositories and provides collaboration, project management, code review and automation features.

Official website: GitHub
What does GitHub provide?
1. Online repository hosting.
2. Team collaboration.
3. Pull Requests.
4. Code reviews.
5. Issues and bug tracking.
6. Project management.
7. GitHub Actions.
8. Security scanning.
9. Release management.
10. Documentation.
Important classroom explanation
Git and GitHub are not the same thing.

| Git | GitHub |
| Version control system | Repository hosting platform |
| Installed on a computer | Cloud-based platform |
| Tracks project changes | Hosts Git repositories |
| Works offline | Enables online collaboration |
| Uses commands such as commit and branch | Provides Pull Requests, Issues and Actions |
| Can work without GitHub | Uses Git repositories |


Understand the analogy:

Git is the engine that manages version history.
GitHub is the platform where developers host and collaborate on those Git repositories.

PART 2 – Understanding GitHub Architecture
2. How GitHub works
This is an important conceptual topic.

Suppose three developers are working on one project.
- Developer A works on the homepage.
- Developer B works on the login page.
- Developer C works on the payment page.
Each developer has a local Git repository.

They synchronize their work with a common GitHub repository.

Understand this architecture:

          DEVELOPER A
          Local Git Repo
                |
                |
                v
          DEVELOPER B
          Local Git Repo
                |
                |
                v
        GITHUB REMOTE REPOSITORY
                ^
                |
                |
          DEVELOPER C
          Local Git Repo

A more accurate way to understand the workflow is:

Developer A                 Developer B
    |                            |
    v                            v
Local Repository            Local Repository
    |                            |
    | Push                       | Pull/Fetch
    |                            |
    +------------+---------------+
                 |
                 v
          GitHub Repository

Each developer can:
- Push commits to GitHub, if authorized.
- Fetch changes from GitHub.
- Pull changes from GitHub.
- Clone the repository.
- Create and manage branches.
Three important terms
Local Repository: The Git repository on your computer.

Remote Repository: A Git repository hosted somewhere else, such as GitHub.

Remote URL: The address used to connect your local repository to the remote repository.

GitHub documentation: About remote repositories

