4. Public vs Private Repository
This is important from a security perspective.
Public Repository	Private Repository
Visible to everyone	Access restricted
Anyone can view public code	Only authorized users can view
Useful for open-source projects	Useful for private projects
Suitable for portfolios	Suitable for confidential projects


Important: Public does not mean everyone can directly push code. Writing access still requires appropriate permissions.
Ask students
If you are developing a company's confidential banking application, should you use a public repository?

Answer: No. A private repository is generally appropriate, subject to the organization's security policies.
