PART 9 – Understanding .gitignore
6. What is .gitignore?
This is one of the most important concepts, particularly for DevSecOps students.

Suppose a developer is working on a Python project.

The project directory contains:
my-project/
│
├── app.py
├── requirements.txt
├── .env
├── __pycache__/
├── venv/
├── debug.log
└── Dockerfile

Should all these files be uploaded to GitHub?

No.

Some files should not be tracked.

For example:
- Password files.
- API keys.
- Environment configuration.
- Temporary files.
- Python cache.
- Virtual environments.
- Log files.
We use .gitignore to tell Git which untracked files and directories to ignore.
Practical 4: Create .gitignore
Create a file:
nano .gitignore

Add:
# Environment variables
.env
.env.*

# Python cache
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Virtual environments
venv/
.venv/

# Log files
*.log

# IDE files
.vscode/
.idea/

# Operating system files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

Save the file.

Now check:
git status

Files matching these ignore rules will generally not appear as untracked files.
Important teaching point
.gitignore normally affects files that are not already tracked by Git.

If a file has already been committed, adding it to .gitignore does not automatically remove it from tracking.

To stop tracking an already tracked file while keeping it locally:
git rm --cached .env

Then commit the removal.

Security warning: Never upload passwords, AWS access keys, private keys or production credentials to GitHub. .gitignore is not a secret-protection system. If a secret has already been pushed, revoke or rotate it immediately and address its presence in Git history.
PART 10 – Understanding Local vs Remote Repository
Now we are reaching the core of today's practical.

Suppose we have:
LOCAL COMPUTER

the-fairy-bake/
    index.html
    about.html
    README.md

And:
GITHUB

the-fairy-bake
    README.md

These are two different repository locations.

We need to connect them.

This connection is established using Git Remote.
7. What is Git Remote?
A remote is a named reference to another Git repository.

For example:
git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/the-fairy-bake.git

Here:
- git remote = Manage remote repositories.
- add = Add a new remote.
- origin = Name assigned to the remote.
- URL = Address of the GitHub repository.
What is origin?
origin is simply a conventional name for the default remote repository.

It is not a special Git keyword.

You could use another name, but origin is widely used.

Explain this analogy:

Imagine you have saved your friend's phone number in your mobile as:
Name: Rahul
Number: 9876543210

Instead of remembering the complete number, you use the name Rahul.

Similarly, Git stores the remote URL under a name such as origin.
PART 11 – Connecting Local Git with GitHub
Practical 5: Push an Existing Local Repository to GitHub
This is the most important practical of today's session.

We will use our existing bakery project.
Step 1. Create a local project
mkdir fairy-bake-local
cd fairy-bake-local

Step 2. Initialize Git
git init

Step 3. Create an HTML file
nano index.html

Add:
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>The Fairy Bake</title>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>

    <p>Fresh cakes and bakery products.</p>

</body>
</html>

Step 4. Create README
nano README.md

Add:
# The Fairy Bake

My first GitHub project.

This project demonstrates how Git and GitHub
are used to manage source code.

Step 5. Stage files
git add .

Step 6. Commit
git commit -m "Initial bakery website"

Step 7. Rename the branch
git branch -M main

Step 8. Create an empty GitHub repository
On GitHub:
1. Click New Repository.
2. Enter fairy-bake-local.
3. Select Public or Private.
4. Do not initialize with README.
5. Do not add a .gitignore.
6. Click Create Repository.
Why are we leaving it empty?

Because our local repository already has commits.

Initializing the remote with a separate README commit would create two unrelated histories and require additional integration.

For this beginner demonstration, an empty remote is simpler.
Step 9. Add the remote URL
Copy the HTTPS URL from GitHub.

It looks like:
https://github.com/USERNAME/fairy-bake-local.git

Execute:
git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/fairy-bake-local.git

Replace USERNAME with your actual GitHub username.
Step 10. Verify the remote
git remote -v

Expected output:
origin  https://github.com/USERNAME/fairy-bake-local.git (fetch)
origin  https://github.com/USERNAME/fairy-bake-local.git (push)

Step 11. Push the code
git push -u origin main

What does this command mean?
Component	Meaning
git push	Upload local commits to remote
-u	Set upstream tracking
origin	Remote repository name
main	Branch to push


After authentication succeeds, your files will appear on GitHub.

Congratulations! You have published your first local Git repository to GitHub.

Official documentation: Managing remote repositories
PART 12 – Understanding Git Push
8. What is Git Push?
Git Push sends local commits to a remote repository.

Suppose you modify the bakery website.

Add:
<h2>Our Special Cakes</h2>

Now:
git status

Stage:
git add .

Commit:
git commit -m "Added special cakes section"

Push:
git push

Now GitHub receives the new commit.
Explain the difference
git commit saves changes in your local repository.

git push publishes those commits to the remote repository.

Important: Git Push uploads commits, not merely files that have been staged.
PART 13 – Understanding Git Clone
9. What is Git Clone?
Imagine another student wants to download your bakery project.

The student does not have the project locally.

What should they do?

They use:
git clone <repository-url>

Git Clone creates a local copy of the remote repository, including its Git history.

Practical 6: Clone a GitHub Repository
Ask students to open another terminal.

Execute:
git clone https://github.com/USERNAME/fairy-bake-local.git

Now:
cd fairy-bake-local

Check:
ls

You should see:
index.html
README.md

Check repository history:
git log --oneline

Check remote:
git remote -v

You will see origin configured automatically.
Important difference
Git Init	Git Clone
Creates a new local repository	Copies an existing repository
Does not automatically connect to GitHub	Automatically configures the cloned remote
Starts with no project history	Downloads existing repository history


Classroom question:

If you want to download an existing project from GitHub, should you use git init or git clone?

Answer: git clone.
PART 14 – Understanding Git Fetch
10. What is Git Fetch?
Suppose Developer A and Developer B are working on the same project.

Developer A pushes a new commit to GitHub.

Developer B wants to know whether new commits are available.

But Developer B does not want to immediately merge those changes into their working branch.

What should Developer B do?

Use:
git fetch

Git Fetch downloads new remote information and updates remote-tracking references.

It does not automatically merge those changes into the current local branch.
Visual explanation
Before Fetch:
Local main:

A ---- B ---- C

GitHub main:
A ---- B ---- C ---- D

After:
Local main:

A ---- B ---- C

origin/main:

A ---- B ---- C ---- D

The remote-tracking branch origin/main now reflects the latest fetched remote state.
Practical
Execute:
git fetch origin

Now check:
git branch -a

You may see:
* main
  remotes/origin/main

To inspect the difference:
git log main..origin/main --oneline

This displays commits present in origin/main but not in local main.

To integrate the changes:
git merge origin/main

PART 15 – Understanding Git Pull
11. What is Git Pull?
Git Pull is used to retrieve and integrate changes from a remote repository into the current local branch.

At a conceptual level:
git pull = git fetch + integration

The integration is normally a merge, unless the pull configuration or command specifies rebase or another strategy.
Practical demonstration
Suppose another student updates the README file on GitHub.

On your local system, execute:
git pull origin main

Git downloads the latest changes and integrates them into your current branch.
Important distinction: Fetch vs Pull
Git Fetch	Git Pull
Downloads remote updates	Downloads and integrates remote updates
Does not merge into current branch	Normally merges into current branch
Gives more control	Convenient for routine synchronization
Useful for inspecting changes first	Useful when ready to integrate changes


Teaching tip: Tell students that Fetch is like checking whether new material is available. Pull is like downloading it and incorporating it into their current work.
