PART 17 – Understanding HTTPS vs SSH Authentication
Now comes an important DevSecOps topic.

When you push code to GitHub, GitHub needs to verify that you have permission to write to the repository.

There are two common connection methods.
1. HTTPS.
2. SSH.
12. HTTPS Authentication
Example repository URL:
https://github.com/USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git

HTTPS is easy to understand and commonly used.

When Git needs authentication, it may use Git Credential Manager or prompt for credentials.

Important: GitHub no longer supports ordinary account passwords for Git operations over HTTPS.

You can authenticate using:
- GitHub CLI and browser authentication.
- Personal Access Token.
- Git Credential Manager.
Official documentation: GitHub authentication methods
13. What is a Personal Access Token (PAT)?
A Personal Access Token is a token used to authenticate with GitHub.

Think of it as a secure credential that grants specified access to GitHub resources.

Instead of using your GitHub account password for HTTPS Git operations, you use a token when prompted for a password.
Types of PAT
GitHub provides two major types:
1. Fine-grained Personal Access Tokens.
2. Personal Access Tokens (classic).
Fine-grained tokens provide more specific repository and permission controls and are generally preferable when supported.
Practical 7: Create a Personal Access Token
Open:

GitHub Personal Access Token Settings

Steps:
1. Log in to GitHub.
2. Open Settings.
3. Navigate to Developer settings.
4. Select Personal access tokens.
5. Select Fine-grained tokens.
6. Click Generate new token.
7. Enter a token name.
8. Set an expiration date.
9. Select the appropriate resource owner.
10. Restrict repository access to the required repository.
11. Grant only the permissions needed.
For a basic repository push practical, the token needs repository Contents permission with Read and write access.
12. Generate the token.
13. Copy it and store it securely.
Very important security instructions:
- Never share your token with students.
- Never put tokens inside source code.
- Never upload tokens to GitHub.
- Never include tokens in screenshots.
- Never store tokens in README files.
If exposed, revoke the token immediately.

When using HTTPS authentication, enter your GitHub username when prompted for username and the PAT when prompted for password.

Git Credential Manager can securely manage credentials and avoid repeated manual entry.

GitHub documentation: Managing Personal Access Tokens
PART 18 – SSH Authentication
This is particularly useful for students who will work with Linux, EC2 and DevSecOps pipelines.
14. What is SSH?
SSH stands for Secure Shell.

It is a network protocol used to securely communicate with remote systems.

GitHub supports SSH authentication through public/private key pairs.
Understand Public and Private Keys
When you generate an SSH key pair, two files are created.
SSH Key Pair
     |
     |
     +---- Private Key
     |
     +---- Public Key

Private Key:
- Remains on your computer.
- Must be protected.
- Must never be shared.
Public Key:
- Can be added to your GitHub account.
- Is used by GitHub to verify the corresponding private key.
How does SSH authentication work?

1. Developer generates an SSH key pair.
2. Developer adds the public key to GitHub.
3. Developer connects using an SSH remote URL.
4. GitHub verifies that the developer possesses the corresponding private key.
5. If authentication and repository permissions are valid, access is granted.
PART 19 – Practical: Configure SSH Authentication
We will perform this practical on Ubuntu and Windows.
Step 1. Check existing SSH keys
Ubuntu:
ls -la ~/.ssh

Windows PowerShell:
Get-ChildItem ~/.ssh

Look for:
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.pub

If these files already exist, do not overwrite them. You can reuse the existing key or create a separate key with another filename.
Step 2. Generate an SSH key
Ubuntu:
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "student@example.com"

Windows PowerShell:
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "student@example.com"

The command will ask where to save the key.

Press Enter to accept the default path if appropriate.

You can set a passphrase for additional protection.

Two files will be generated:
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.pub

Step 3. Start SSH Agent
On Ubuntu/Git Bash:
eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"

Add the private key:
ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

On Windows PowerShell, you can use the Windows OpenSSH agent service. If necessary, open PowerShell as Administrator:
Get-Service ssh-agent
Set-Service -Name ssh-agent -StartupType Automatic
Start-Service ssh-agent

Then, in your normal PowerShell terminal:
ssh-add "$env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519"

Step 4. Copy the public key
Ubuntu/Git Bash:
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

Windows PowerShell:
Get-Content "$env:USERPROFILE\.ssh\id_ed25519.pub"

Copy the entire output.

Never copy or upload id_ed25519 (the private key).
Step 5. Add the public key to GitHub
Open:

GitHub SSH and GPG Keys Settings

Steps:
1. Open GitHub Settings.
2. Select SSH and GPG keys.
3. Click New SSH key.
4. Enter a title, such as Student Windows Laptop.
5. Select Authentication Key.
6. Paste your public key.
7. Click Add SSH key.
Step 6. Test the connection
Execute:
ssh -T git@github.com

On first connection, verify the host fingerprint against GitHub's published SSH fingerprints before accepting it.

Successful authentication will produce a greeting identifying your GitHub username.
Step 7. Use SSH repository URL
An SSH repository URL looks like:
git@github.com:USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git

To change an existing repository from HTTPS to SSH:
git remote set-url origin git@github.com:USERNAME/REPOSITORY.git

Verify:
git remote -v

Now:
git push

Your SSH key will be used for authentication.

Official documentation: Connecting to GitHub with SSH
