PART 22 – Troubleshooting Common GitHub Errors
These are errors your students will frequently encounter.
Error 1: Remote origin already exists
fatal: remote origin already exists.

Reason: A remote named origin is already configured.

Solution:
git remote -v

If the URL is incorrect:
git remote set-url origin <new-url>

Error 2: Authentication failed
Authentication failed

Possible reasons:
- Incorrect credentials.
- Expired or revoked PAT.
- Incorrect token permissions.
- Cached invalid credentials.
Solution:

Verify authentication and permissions. For HTTPS, use Git Credential Manager or a valid PAT.
Error 3: Repository not found
ERROR: Repository not found.

Possible reasons:
- Incorrect repository URL.
- Repository does not exist.
- Repository is private and you lack access.
- Wrong GitHub account is being used.
Error 4: Rejected push
! [rejected] main -> main (fetch first)

Reason:

The remote repository contains commits that your local branch does not have.

Solution:
git pull --rebase origin main

Resolve any conflicts if they occur, then:
git push origin main

Explain that --rebase replays your local commits on top of the updated remote history. If the team uses merge-based integration, git pull origin main may be more appropriate.
Error 5: Permission denied (publickey)
Permission denied (publickey).

Possible reasons:
- Public key not added to GitHub.
- Wrong private key loaded.
- SSH agent not running.
- Incorrect SSH remote URL.
Check:
ssh -T git@github.com

Verify your key configuration.
